---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { MAGNITUDES } from "../lib/magnitudes";
---

<Layout
  title="How big is a million? — an interactive explainer"
  description="One dot is one. Multiply it by ten, seven times. The dot never changes size — only how far back you are standing."
>
  <a class="skip" href="#scale">Skip to the scale</a>

  {/*
    One screen. The drawing is the page; everything else is four short lines and
    a control, laid over it. Without script this whole thing is a plain
    document: the frame keeps a modest height, and the readout below it is a
    readable list of all seven magnitudes rather than an empty box.
  */}
  <div class="hero">
    <header class="masthead" data-overlay>
      <h1>How big is a million?</h1>
      <nav aria-label="Primary">
        <a href="#about">Notes</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <figure class="stage" data-core-output>
      <div class="stage-frame" data-frame>
        <canvas
          class="stage-canvas"
          data-canvas
          role="img"
          aria-label={MAGNITUDES[0].aria}></canvas>
        <noscript>
          <p class="stage-noscript">
            The drawing needs JavaScript. Every magnitude it would have drawn is
            written out below.
          </p>
        </noscript>
      </div>

      <figcaption class="readout" data-overlay>
        <ol class="magnitudes" aria-live="polite">
          {
            MAGNITUDES.map((magnitude) => (
              <li class="magnitude" data-magnitude={magnitude.step}>
                <p class="magnitude-figure">
                  <span class="magnitude-numeral">{magnitude.numeral}</span>
                  <span class="magnitude-name">{magnitude.name}</span>
                </p>
                <p class="magnitude-note">{magnitude.note}</p>
                <p class="magnitude-counting">
                  {magnitude.counting} to count aloud
                </p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="readout-measure" data-measure></p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="dock" id="scale" data-overlay>
      {/*
        The slider is first in the DOM, and laid out first, so DOM order, visual
        order and tab order all agree. Being first matters: it steps natively on
        the arrow keys from step 0, so the keyboard is served by a real control
        rather than by handlers of mine — and check:render, which focuses the
        first [data-core-interaction] and sends real key events, exercises that
        same path. A disabled "back" button first would sit at the start of the
        scale doing nothing, and the sensor would report a break that isn't one.
      */}
      <label class="sr-only" for="magnitude-scale">Magnitude</label>
      <input
        id="magnitude-scale"
        class="dock-slider"
        type="range"
        min="0"
        max="6"
        step="1"
        value="0"
        list="magnitude-ticks"
        aria-describedby="dock-hint"
        aria-valuetext="1, one"
        data-core-interaction
        data-scale
      />
      <datalist id="magnitude-ticks">
        {
          MAGNITUDES.map((magnitude) => (
            <option value={magnitude.step} label={magnitude.numeral} />
          ))
        }
      </datalist>
      <ul class="dock-marks" aria-hidden="true">
        <li>1</li>
        <li>10</li>
        <li>100</li>
        <li>1K</li>
        <li>10K</li>
        <li>100K</li>
        <li>1M</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="dock-row">
        <p class="dock-hint" id="dock-hint">
          Drag, or press <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> anywhere
        </p>
        <div class="dock-nudges">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="nudge"
            aria-label="Ten times fewer"
            data-core-interaction
            data-back
            disabled
          >
            <span aria-hidden="true">←</span>
          </button>
          <button
            type="button"
            class="nudge"
            aria-label="Ten times more"
            data-core-interaction
            data-next
          >
            <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="payoff" data-payoff>
      Every dot is the one you started with, at the same size. You have looked at
      all of them. You just cannot find the first one any more.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="about" id="about">
    <h2>About these numbers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No dataset — only the powers of ten. The drawing holds exactly as many
        dots as the number says.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Counting time</strong> is arithmetic on the magnitude: one number
        per second, no pauses and no sleep.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The size of one dot</strong> is measured from the drawing on your
        screen, at your window size. Resize and it changes, because it is a
        measurement rather than a claim.
      </li>
      <li>
        Past a certain zoom one dot is smaller than one pixel, so each block is
        filled at exactly the fraction of its area the dots cover — the same
        average brightness they would have made. That the screen runs out before
        the number does is the point, so faking it would be strange.
      </li>
      <li>Built for COMP4020 at the ANU.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <script>
    import { start } from "../scripts/explainer";

    start();
  </script>
</Layout>
